import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research — Shruti Joshi"
  description="Identifiability without a ground truth: affordances, reachability under optimisation, and the interpretability of language models."
  compact
>
  <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50">
    Research
  </h1>

  <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
    <p>

        My thesis develops affordance-based identifiability for representation learning, in which the learner makes interventions and collects their own training data to learn abstractions of it that preserve the distinctions revealed by those interventions, without requiring inversion of a predefined generative process. The goal is to formalise these learned abstractions that need not correspond to a fixed set of objects or predefined concepts, but instead capture affordances, i.e., how the learner can interact with a system and the reproducible effects of those interactions. This connects representation learning to experimental design, where I study developing policies that enable the learner to select interventions that distinguish competing abstractions consistent with its current evidence. This work complements the study of generalisation error in scaling theory by developing criteria for evaluating the representations themselves.
      Identifiability theory says when a learned representation recovers the structure that
      generated the data, and up to which transformations. The results are stated for settings
      where the true factors are known and can be compared against. My work asks what the theory
      becomes when that comparison is unavailable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="rule mt-8 pt-5">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Identifiability without a ground truth</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
      <p>
        Every identifiability result fixes an equivalence class: the set of representations that
        count as having recovered the same thing. Fixing that class requires saying what the true
        factors are. On a synthetic benchmark the generative process is written down first, so the
        class comes for free. In the settings I care about — the activations of a trained language
        model, the sensory stream of an agent — the factors are unobservable and their number is
        not well defined.
      </p>
      <p>
        The consequence shows up first in evaluation. MCC, R² and DCI are the metrics used to
        certify that recovery has happened, and each one quietly assumes something about the
        data-generating process and something about the encoder.
        <a href="/publications#joshi2026whoguards">Who Guards the Guardians?</a> (UAI 2026)
        separates the two kinds of assumption, characterises the domain on which each metric is
        valid, exhibits the systematic false positives and false negatives that occur outside it,
        and releases a suite for stress testing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule mt-8 pt-5">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Affordances fix the equivalence class</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
      <p>
        I am formalising a different source for the equivalence class: what an agent can do, and
        what it observes as a consequence. Two representations are equivalent when they license the
        same interventions and predict the same outcomes for them. What the definition needs is a
        specified repertoire of actions. It needs no privileged coordinate system and no count of
        true factors.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am developing this in the setting of an agent that learns physical mechanisms by acting
        on its environment, where the repertoire is given by the agent's body and the outcomes are
        observed directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule mt-8 pt-5">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Reachability under finite data and a given optimiser</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
      <p>
        An identifiability theorem is a population-level statement about the global optimum of a
        criterion. Training is finite data, a chosen parameterisation, and an optimiser with its own
        bias. Whether the identifiable solution is reached at all is a separate question, and it is
        the question that decides whether any of the theory is usable.
      </p>
      <p>
        I treat identifiability error the way generalisation error is treated in supervised
        learning: a quantity that varies with sample size, width, and optimisation, and that may
        admit a scaling law. Constrained optimisation is the part I have evidence for so far, and it
        stabilises hyperparameter transfer across scale and across datasets. The open problem is
        characterising how parameterisation, architecture, and optimiser bias jointly determine
        whether identifiable solutions are reachable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule mt-8 pt-5">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Interpretability of language models</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
      <p>
        Interpretability is where I test the framework. A claim about a feature or a circuit is a
        claim that a particular variable has been recovered from a model's activations, so the
        question of what is recoverable, and under which assumptions, is the same question.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pearl's hierarchy supplies the discipline. Observations license associations between
        behaviour and internal components. Interventions such as ablation and activation patching
        license claims about the effect of an edit on a behavioural metric. Counterfactual claims,
        which ask what a model would have done on the same prompt under an intervention that was
        never run, require controlled supervision that most interpretability studies do not have.
        <a href="/publications#joshi2026causalitykeyinterpretabilityclaims">Causality is Key for Interpretability Claims to Generalise</a>
        (ICML 2026) sets this out and shows how causal representation learning specifies which
        variables are recoverable from activations, and under what assumptions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/publications#joshi2025identifiable">Sparse Shift Autoencoders</a> are the method
        that follows. The sparsity constraint applies to the differences between paired embeddings.
        That is enough for identifiability from pairs of observations varying in several unknown
        concepts, which gives steering of one concept at a time with no contrastive supervision.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two studies measure the distance between that standard and current practice.
        <a href="/publications#pacela2026stopprobing">Stop Probing, Start Coding</a> (UAI 2026)
        attributes the failure of sparse autoencoders on unseen concept combinations to the gap
        between amortised and per-sample inference, and shows that the gap survives more training
        data. <a href="/publications#mueller2025isolationentanglement">From Isolation to Entanglement</a>
        holds the correlations between textual concepts under control and finds that sparse
        autoencoder features move many concepts at once when steered.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rule mt-8 pt-5">
    <h2 class="section-heading">In progress</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-[0.95rem]">
      <p>
        <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Jailbreak mechanisms.</strong>
        A jailbreak is a dense perturbation in text space, and the corresponding change in latent
        space should be sparse, which puts it within reach of an identifiable decomposition. The
        question is where safety-relevant information sits in a network, and at which level of
        abstraction it can be controlled.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-100">Multi-agent cooperation.</strong>
        When language models act as agents in social dilemmas, I want to know whether they encode
        behavioural strategies — cooperation, defection, reciprocity — as vectors that survive a
        change of model family and of dialogue context.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-8 text-sm">
    <a href="/publications">All publications</a>
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
